---
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../config";
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Affiliate Disclosure | TripAxle"
  description="How TripAxle uses (and does not use) affiliate links, and why an affiliate relationship never changes the guidance."
  heading="Affiliate Disclosure"
  updated={site.legalUpdated}
>
  <p>
    This is a plain-English disclosure of how TripAxle is, or may in future be,
    supported.
  </p>

  <h2>Current status</h2>
  <p>
    Right now, TripAxle carries <strong>no affiliate links and no advertising</strong>.
    Nothing on the site earns a commission today. This page is published ahead of
    that so the policy is clear from the start.
  </p>

  <h2>What will change, and what won't</h2>
  <p>
    At some point we expect to add a small number of links to car-rental
    comparison services — the kind of link that genuinely helps once you have
    decided a rental car is right for your trip. Some of those may be
    <strong>affiliate links</strong>: if you follow one and go on to book, the
    service may pay TripAxle a commission. It costs you nothing extra, and it
    does not change the price you are offered.
  </p>
  <p>What an affiliate relationship will never do:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Change what a guide says.</strong> The advice, the "when renting
      does not make sense" sections, and the cautions stay exactly as they are.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Buy a ranking or a recommendation.</strong> TripAxle does not rank
      or score rental companies and does not take payment for coverage,
      placement, or reviews.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Decide which link appears.</strong> We only add a commercial link
      where comparing options actually helps the reader act on a decision they
      have already reached.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How links are labelled</h2>
  <p>
    Any commercial or affiliate link will be clearly presented as one — set apart
    from the guidance, and marked in the page code (<code>rel="sponsored"</code>)
    so browsers and search engines can see it too. Ordinary links to airports,
    rental companies, and official sources are just references and earn nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Why trust this</h2>
  <p>{site.trust.whyTrust}</p>
  <p>
    More on our approach in <a href="/about">About &amp; how we research</a>. If
    something reads like it was written to sell you a booking rather than help
    you make one, <a href="/contact">tell us</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
